# English translations of the six tool guides (indices 755 to 760)
T={
755:r"""<p class="gcr">Slack <span>›</span> private list &lt;Name&gt; OKR Tasks <span>›</span> public canvas 🎯 OKR Dashboard</p><p>OKRs link everyone's work to the company goal. Each person only updates their private list; every week, Claude reads all the lists and updates the public dashboard.</p><div class="gflow"><div><b>1 · Each employee</b><span>Updates their tasks and status in their private list</span></div><div><b>2 · Claude (weekly)</b><span>Reads all the private lists and calculates progress</span></div><div><b>3 · Public canvas</b><span>The dashboard shows team and company progress</span></div></div><h5>Step 1 · Update your private OKR list<span class="who">Everyone</span></h5><p>Each employee has a private list <b>&lt;Name&gt; OKR Tasks</b>, visible only to them, their manager and the person who compiles the dashboard. One row per task:</p><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What to enter</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual KPI</td><td>The task or key result</td><td>Set up OKRs in Slack</td></tr><tr><td>Team's objectives</td><td>The team objective the task contributes to</td><td>Migrate to Slack</td></tr><tr><td>Contribution to Team's objectives</td><td>Weight of the task in the objective (%)</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Not started, In progress or Done</td><td>Done</td></tr><tr><td>Last Updated</td><td>Date of the last update</td><td>15/09/2026</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h5>Step 2 · Claude updates the dashboard every week<span class="who">Managers</span></h5><p>Nothing to publish: every week, Claude reads the private lists and recalculates the <b>🎯 OKR Dashboard</b>, a public canvas everyone can see. It shows overall progress, then each team's weight, status and contribution, then each team's objectives and members.</p><figure><img src="guides/okr-00.jpg" alt="The public canvas: company goal (blurred), overall progress and team table." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The public canvas: company goal (blurred), overall progress and team table.</figcaption></figure><h5>The rules</h5><figure><img src="guides/okr-01.jpg" alt="The rule shown on the dashboard." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The rule shown on the dashboard.</figcaption></figure><ul class="gl"><li>Only three things are entered by hand: your own tasks (in your private list), the name of each objective (by the team manager) and the weight of each team (by leadership).</li><li>Everything else is calculated by Claude: every status and every contribution, at every level.</li><li>An objective switches to Done when all its tasks are finished; otherwise it stays “not set yet”.</li><li>An empty list means “no data yet”, not a zero contribution.</li></ul>""",
756:r"""<p class="gcr">Slack <span>›</span> workflow PTO request <span>›</span> Google Calendar</p><p>Time off is requested through a short Slack form. The manager approves or declines right in Slack, everyone is notified automatically and approved time off is added to Google Calendar.</p><div class="gflow"><div><b>1 · Employee</b><span>Starts the workflow and fills in the form</span></div><div><b>2 · Manager</b><span>Gets a direct message and clicks Respond</span></div><div><b>3 · Time-off channel</b><span>A summary of the request and the decision is posted</span></div><div><b>4 · Employee</b><span>Gets the decision and a link to the calendar</span></div><div><b>5 · Google Calendar</b><span>The approved time off is added to the shared calendar</span></div></div><h5>Employee side: request time off<span class="who">Everyone</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>Open the <b>PTO request</b> workflow link and click <b>Start</b>.</li><li>Fill in: PTO start date, PTO end date, Who is your manager?, Notes (optional) and Half-day? (AM or PM, for a single day). Then submit.</li><li>A direct message says whether the request is approved or declined, with two buttons: <b>See your request on calendar</b> and <b>Submit a new request</b>.</li></ol><h5>Manager side: approve or decline<span class="who">Managers</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>The manager gets a direct message: “[Name] is requesting PTO. Please approve or deny.”, with the dates and notes.</li><li>They click <b>Respond</b>, choose <b>Approve</b> or <b>Deny</b>, add a note if needed and confirm. The employee and the channel are updated automatically.</li></ol><figure><img src="guides/pto-00.jpg" alt="The time-off channel: each request is logged with its dates, half-day, and who approved or declined it." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The time-off channel: each request is logged with its dates, half-day, and who approved or declined it.</figcaption></figure><h5>Approved time off in Google Calendar</h5><p>Once approved, the request is automatically added to the shared Google calendar as a <b>PTO, &lt;Name&gt;</b> event. Full days show as “all day” and half-days as a morning or afternoon block. The <b>See your request on calendar</b> button opens this calendar. Declined requests don't appear.</p><figure><img src="guides/pto-01.jpg" alt="The shared calendar: two days off and a half-day (PM) approved. Names replaced." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The shared calendar: two days off and a half-day (PM) approved. Names replaced.</figcaption></figure><h5>How the automation works<span class="who">HR</span></h5><p>The workflow has five steps: it collects the request, asks the manager, collects the decision, posts the result in the channel and notifies the employee.</p><div class="g2"><figure><img src="guides/pto-02.jpg" alt="Steps 1 and 2: request form, then a message to the chosen manager." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Steps 1 and 2: request form, then a message to the chosen manager.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/pto-03.jpg" alt="Steps 3 and 4: the manager's Approve/Deny form, then the summary in the channel." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Steps 3 and 4: the manager's Approve/Deny form, then the summary in the channel.</figcaption></figure></div><figure><img src="guides/pto-04.jpg" alt="Step 5: the employee is told the decision, with links to the calendar and a new request." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Step 5: the employee is told the decision, with links to the calendar and a new request.</figcaption></figure>""",
757:r"""<p class="gcr">Notion <span>›</span> HR space <span>›</span> 🌴 Contractors Calendar</p><p>A single page where contractors request time off and managers approve it. It holds a how-to (one section for contractors, one for managers), the request form and the calendar of approved absences. A public holiday calendar sits further down the page.</p><figure><img src="guides/contractors-00.jpg" alt="The page: how-to at the top, form on the left, team calendar on the right." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The page: how-to at the top, form on the left, team calendar on the right.</figcaption></figure><div class="gflow"><div><b>1 · Contractor</b><span>Submits the request form</span></div><div><b>2 · Automatic email</b><span>Confirmation to the contractor, action link to the manager</span></div><div><b>3 · Manager</b><span>Clicks Approve or Deny</span></div><div><b>4 · Automatic email</b><span>Decision sent; the approved absence appears in the calendar</span></div></div><h5>Contractor side: make a request<span class="who">Contractors</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>Open the <b>🌴 Contractors Calendar</b> page.</li><li>Fill in the form: name, work email, manager's name and email, start and end date.<figure><img src="guides/contractors-01.jpg" alt="The form (left) and the calendar where approved requests appear (right)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The form (left) and the calendar where approved requests appear (right).</figcaption></figure></li><li><b>Half-day?</b> Choose AM or PM only for a single-day request. If a longer leave starts or ends with a half-day, declare it in a separate request.</li><li>Click <b>Submit</b>.<figure><img src="guides/contractors-02.jpg" alt="Bottom of the form: half-day option and Submit button." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Bottom of the form: half-day option and Submit button.</figcaption></figure></li><li>A confirmation email arrives right away, then a second email with the manager's decision.</li></ol><h5>Manager side: approve or decline<span class="who">Managers</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>The manager gets an email for each new request and clicks the link.</li><li>They click <b>Approve</b> or <b>Deny</b>. The contractor is notified automatically by email.</li></ol><h5>Automatic email notifications</h5><p>All emails are sent by a Notion automation (marked “Sent via Notion Automations”). Don't reply to them.</p><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Email subject</th><th>Recipients</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Request sent</td><td>Leave Request Submitted [Name · dates]</td><td>Contractor, manager, HR</td><td>Confirms the dates and that the request is under review. The manager's part holds the New submission link to act on it.</td></tr><tr><td>Request approved</td><td>Leave Request Approved [Name · dates]</td><td>Contractor (manager and HR in copy)</td><td>Who approved, the dates, the number of days and a reminder to prepare the handover.</td></tr><tr><td>Request approved</td><td>&lt;Name&gt; Time Off Balance Notification</td><td>Contractor (HR in copy)</td><td>Total leave entitlement and remaining balance.</td></tr><tr><td>Request declined</td><td>Leave Request Declined [Name · dates]</td><td>Contractor and manager (HR in copy)</td><td>The decline, with the details of the dates.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="g2"><figure><img src="guides/contractors-03.jpg" alt="Request sent: confirmation to the contractor and action link for the manager." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Request sent: confirmation to the contractor and action link for the manager.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/contractors-04.jpg" alt="Request approved." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Request approved.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/contractors-05.jpg" alt="Leave balance (figures hidden)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Leave balance (figures hidden).</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/contractors-06.jpg" alt="Request declined." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Request declined.</figcaption></figure></div>""",
758:r"""<p class="gcr">Slack <span>›</span> section 💙 Onboarding Hub <span>›</span> #firstname-onboarding</p><p>Each newcomer gets a private channel: a <b>Messages</b> tab to ask questions and a task tab (a Slack List) with their whole onboarding roadmap.</p><h5>Step 1 · Create the channel with the workflow<span class="who">HR</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>Open the <b>👋 How to create an Onboarding channel</b> canvas and click <b>Start Workflow</b>, then fill in the short form.<figure><img src="guides/onboarding-00.jpg" alt="The “How to create an Onboarding channel” canvas and its Start Workflow button." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The “How to create an Onboarding channel” canvas and its Start Workflow button.</figcaption></figure></li><li>The workflow creates a private channel <b>onboarding-&lt;date&gt;</b>, sets the topic to “onboarding”, then posts and pins a welcome message with two buttons: <b>Read the Guide</b> and <b>Book a 30min meeting</b> (to meet colleagues).<figure><img src="guides/onboarding-01.jpg" alt="Behind the scenes: the workflow sends the welcome message, pins it, then sends the 30-minute meeting invite." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Behind the scenes: the workflow sends the welcome message, pins it, then sends the 30-minute meeting invite.</figcaption></figure></li><li>Rename the channel to <b>firstname-onboarding</b>, add the newcomer, their manager and their buddy, then file it in the Onboarding Hub section.</li></ol><h5>Step 2 · Add the onboarding template<span class="who">HR</span></h5><ol class="gs"><li>In the channel, click <b>+</b> in the tab bar, then <b>Explore more templates</b>.<figure><img src="guides/onboarding-02.jpg" alt="The tab bar's “+” menu › Explore more templates." loading="lazy"><figcaption>The tab bar's “+” menu › Explore more templates.</figcaption></figure></li><li>Choose the template that matches the role (for example <b>[Onboarding] Engineering</b> or <b>QA onboarding</b>), check the preview and click <b>Next</b>.<figure><img src="guides/onboarding-03.jpg" alt="Choosing the template; the preview shows the task tab that will be added." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Choosing the template; the preview shows the task tab that will be added.</figcaption></figure></li></ol><figure><img src="guides/onboarding-04.jpg" alt="Example task tab after applying the template: tasks are blurred, only the step groups are visible." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Example task tab after applying the template: tasks are blurred, only the step groups are visible.</figcaption></figure><h5>Step 3 · How the newcomer uses it<span class="who">Newcomers</span></h5><p><b>Messages tab.</b> For every question: organisation, admin, tools. HR, the manager and the buddy are in the channel. The pinned message links to the guide and an intro meeting.</p><figure><img src="guides/onboarding-05.jpg" alt="Messages tab: pinned welcome message with “Read the Guide” and “Book a 30min meeting”." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Messages tab: pinned welcome message with “Read the Guide” and “Book a 30min meeting”.</figcaption></figure><p><b>Onboarding tab.</b> The roadmap, grouped by step (for example 0. First impressions report, 1. My first weeks…). Each task has:</p><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Task</td><td>What to do. Tick the circle once done. A “1/2” counter shows subtasks.</td></tr><tr><td>File</td><td>The guide or document needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Not started, In progress or Done.</td></tr><tr><td>Checkpoint</td><td>The onboarding step the task belongs to.</td></tr><tr><td>Owner</td><td>The person responsible for the task.</td></tr><tr><td>Due date</td><td>Deadline, calculated automatically: nobody has to enter dates.</td></tr><tr><td>Channel</td><td>The relevant Slack channel, if any.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure><img src="guides/onboarding-06.jpg" alt="Onboarding tab: tasks by step, with automatic due dates." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Onboarding tab: tasks by step, with automatic due dates.</figcaption></figure>""",
759:r"""<p class="gcr">Notion <span>›</span> HR space <span>›</span> Hiring Hub</p><p>The Hiring Hub brings every step of recruitment together in one place, in three cards. Each card says who it's for and what it's for.</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-00.jpg" alt="Hiring Hub home: ① Hiring brief, ② ATS (candidate tracking), ③ Talent pool." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Hiring Hub home: ① Hiring brief, ② ATS (candidate tracking), ③ Talent pool.</figcaption></figure><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>For whom</th><th>What it's for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>① Hiring brief, JD, Scorecard</td><td>Hiring manager</td><td>Fill in the brief before the role opens.</td></tr><tr><td>② Applicant Trackers (ATS)</td><td>HR</td><td>Track every application, role by role.</td></tr><tr><td>③ Talent Pool</td><td>HR</td><td>Find past candidates or spontaneous applications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h5>① Write a hiring brief<span class="who">Hiring managers</span></h5><p>Briefs are sorted by department. To create one, click the small arrow next to <b>New</b>, choose the <b>Hiring Brief [Job_Title]</b> template (the default), then rename it with the job title.</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-01.jpg" alt="HIRING BRIEF database, grouped by department (“Per department” view)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>HIRING BRIEF database, grouped by department (“Per department” view).</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/hiring-02.jpg" alt="New ▾ › Templates for HIRING BRIEF › Hiring Brief [Job_Title]." loading="lazy"><figcaption>New ▾ › Templates for HIRING BRIEF › Hiring Brief [Job_Title].</figcaption></figure><p>The template guides the manager through eight sections:</p><ol class="gs"><li>Role overview (start date, contract, location, salary range, reason for hiring)</li><li>Experience and seniority</li><li>Expected results (at 6 and 12 months)</li><li>Technical skills</li><li>Soft skills and culture fit</li><li>What we offer and motivations we look for</li><li>Recruitment process</li><li>Free notes</li></ol><p>The job description is then generated from the brief (<b>JD Generated</b> field).</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-03.jpg" alt="Example brief (salary blurred)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Example brief (salary blurred).</figcaption></figure><h5>② Track candidates (ATS)<span class="who">HR</span></h5><p>Open the department section and click the role's button (for example <b>➕ Senior SWE</b>) to reach its tracker. The Applicant Tracker table lists each role with its status, dates, Tally application form and linked brief.</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-04.jpg" alt="Applicant Trackers: role buttons by department." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Applicant Trackers: role buttons by department.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/hiring-05.jpg" alt="Applicant Tracker table: status (Not started, In progress, Done), Tally form and linked brief." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Applicant Tracker table: status (Not started, In progress, Done), Tally form and linked brief.</figcaption></figure><p>Each role has its own tracking page. Candidates appear as cards, sorted by interview stage, with their AssessFirst score, their scorecard and checkboxes for invitations.</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-06.jpg" alt="A role's tracking page, with its warning banner." loading="lazy"><figcaption>A role's tracking page, with its warning banner.</figcaption></figure><div class="gnote"><b>Don't change a candidate's stage by hand.</b> As the banner says, stages are managed automatically: don't move cards between columns.</div><figure><img src="guides/hiring-07.jpg" alt="Candidate board by stage (names blurred)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Candidate board by stage (names blurred).</figcaption></figure><h5>③ Browse the talent pool<span class="who">HR</span></h5><p>The Talent Pool holds every candidate from the last 24 months, sorted by department. The default view shows spontaneous applications; the <b>All Data</b> view shows everyone, with filters by form or date.</p><figure><img src="guides/hiring-08.jpg" alt="Talent Pool (candidate data blurred)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Talent Pool (candidate data blurred).</figcaption></figure>""",
760:r"""<p class="gcr">Notion <span>›</span> HR space <span>›</span> Annual Review <span>›</span> ☀️ Annual Review</p><p>The annual review relies on three forms. Each person picks the one that matches their role. Answers can be in French or English.</p><figure><img src="guides/annual-00.jpg" alt="Starting point: “I am an Employee”, “I am a Manager” or “I am a colleague”." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Starting point: “I am an Employee”, “I am a Manager” or “I am a colleague”.</figcaption></figure><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Filled in by</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>💻 I am an Employee</td><td>The employee, about themselves</td><td>Name, email, manager's email, then 10 questions: goals achieved (last 6 months), successes, difficulties, fulfilment, goals for the next 6 months, needs (tools, training…), outlook, what the manager should keep doing, what to improve, other topics.</td></tr><tr><td>💼 I am a Manager</td><td>The manager, for each team member</td><td>The same themes from the manager's point of view: goals achieved, successes, difficulties observed, engagement and priority goals.</td></tr><tr><td>🥖 I am a colleague</td><td>The peers asked for feedback</td><td>Email of the colleague being reviewed and of their manager, then statements on quality of work, expertise, time management, communication and helpfulness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="g3"><figure><img src="guides/annual-01.jpg" alt="💻 Employee form" loading="lazy"><figcaption>💻 Employee form</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-02.jpg" alt="💼 Manager form" loading="lazy"><figcaption>💼 Manager form</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-03.jpg" alt="🥖 Peer form" loading="lazy"><figcaption>🥖 Peer form</figcaption></figure></div><h5>After submitting</h5><div class="gflow"><div><b>Employee form</b><span>Sent by the employee</span></div><div><b>Automatic email</b><span>Copy of the answers to the employee and their manager</span></div><div><b>Manager form</b><span>Sent by the manager</span></div><div><b>Automatic email</b><span>Copy of the answers to the manager</span></div><div><b>Debrief done</b><span>The employee receives the manager's feedback</span></div></div><ol class="gs"><li>The employee submits their form. A copy of the answers goes to the employee and their manager.</li><li>The manager submits their form. A copy of the answers is sent to them as confirmation.</li><li>The debrief meeting takes place. When HR marks it as done, the employee receives their manager's feedback by email.</li></ol><h5>Automatic email notifications</h5><p>All emails are sent by Notion automations (“Sent via Notion Automations”), with HR in copy.</p><div class="gtw"><table class="gt"><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>Email subject</th><th>Recipients</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The employee submits their form</td><td>Confirmation: &lt;Employee&gt;'s Annual Review Submission</td><td>Employee and manager</td><td>Confirms the submission and repeats all the employee's answers.</td></tr><tr><td>The manager submits their form</td><td>Following &lt;Employee&gt; Annual Review Debrief Meeting</td><td>Manager</td><td>Confirms it was saved, repeats their answers and invites them to prepare the debrief.</td></tr><tr><td>A colleague submits feedback</td><td>&lt;Colleague&gt; Feedback submitted: Annual Review</td><td>The colleague</td><td>Confirms the feedback was saved, with the answers.</td></tr><tr><td>HR marks the debrief as done</td><td>Annual Review: Feedback from your manager</td><td>Employee</td><td>Thanks them for the review and shares the manager's answers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="g2"><figure><img src="guides/annual-04.jpg" alt="Employee submission: confirmation to the employee and their manager." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Employee submission: confirmation to the employee and their manager.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-05.jpg" alt="Manager submission: confirmation and debrief preparation." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Manager submission: confirmation and debrief preparation.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-06.jpg" alt="Peer feedback: confirmation it was saved." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Peer feedback: confirmation it was saved.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-07.jpg" alt="After the debrief: the employee receives the final feedback." loading="lazy"><figcaption>After the debrief: the employee receives the final feedback.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="gnote"><b>Annual Review: Personal Dashboard</b> (under the three buttons) only shows the reviews that concern you.</div><h5>Behind the scenes<span class="who">HR</span></h5><p>Each form creates a page in one of three databases: <b>For employees</b> and <b>For managers</b> (grouped by manager) and <b>Peer feedback</b> (grouped by employee). HR edits the questions in the Form builder view.</p><figure><img src="guides/annual-08.jpg" alt="“Annual Review, For employees”, grouped by manager (names blurred)." loading="lazy"><figcaption>“Annual Review, For employees”, grouped by manager (names blurred).</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="guides/annual-09.jpg" alt="“For managers” and “Peer feedback” databases." loading="lazy"><figcaption>“For managers” and “Peer feedback” databases.</figcaption></figure>""",
}
